<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tripartitum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1514">1514</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tripartitum opus iuris consuetudinarii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tripartitum is the compilation of the customary law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istvan-werboczy">István Werbőczy</a> and presented to the diet in 1514. Though never formally promulgated, it was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1517 and used by the courts as the basic law book, confirming the equal privileges of the nobility and the subjection of the peasants. It remained in force until 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tripartitum.jpg" alt="Tripartitum" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Werbőczy István, Ivanuš Pergošć · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripartitum_1574.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Jagiellonian kings Vladislaus II and Louis II the great magnates regained control and royal revenues collapsed, while the lawyer István Werbőczy compiled the customary law of the nobility in the <b>Tripartitum</b> of 1514, which remained the basis of Hungarian noble law until 1848.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
